89:- **Loading, for now.** Every registered tool loads on every turn in both adapters: the in-app adapter sends the full function list with each request, and MCP `tools/list` returns the whole catalog. This is the simplest correct behaviour and it keeps the tools array byte-identical across a chat, which is what OpenAI's prompt cache keys on. The budget of 20 definitions per turn is a measured trigger, not a gate: the live regression records definition bytes, input tokens, and cached tokens per request, and the journey catalog below will exceed 20 definitions from its first milestone. That is accepted until those measurements show selection errors or context pressure.
396:**M3 eval, 2026-09-16, Windows, fake Bambu A1 mini.** Over MCP (the scripted client in place of Claude Code, approvals through the page's decision hook): a 1 × 0.5 × 0.25 block written in inches was imported (Orca asked "Object too small", answered no), `object_analyze` reported `unitsSuspicion: inches`, `unitsFix` made it 25.4 × 12.7 × 6.35 mm under a new id, the 25.4 × 6.35 face was put down by handle, quantity 2 and arrange all finished with nothing unplaced, and `fit` put both copies inside the plate with the 161.29 mm² face on the bed — pass. In-app with OpenAI and the file attached to the chat, five runs, reviewed per the handoff:
400:- Runs 3 to 5 used the right tools in the right order (import, analyze, place face down, layout with quantity and arrange) and the arrange job left nothing unplaced; both copies were inside the plate each time. The face was wrong in two of the three: once the model named the right handle and a later check still found the end face down (not reproduced by any scripted sequence, arrange with or without rotation included), and once it chose the face already on the bed while saying otherwise. Faces now report `sizeMm` (their two sides), which is how a person names them. This remains an eval failure against the pinned model and is recorded as such.
419:**M5 eval, 2026-09-17, Windows, fake Bambu A1 mini.** Over MCP (the scripted client, approvals through the page's decision hook), on `tests/data/jusprin/tee_with_hole.stl` opened as a project: normal supports on everywhere; the first slice's report found support inside the unannotated hole (3,945.6 mm² over 37 layers); `object_analyze` features gave the 10 mm hole and the back face; `region_annotate` made the hole a precision hole and the back face hidden, both reported bound with their artifacts; the second slice's report had no support contact and put all 227 seams on the hidden face — pass. No dialog appeared in either eval (a watcher sampled every visible window).
439:**What the journey eval changed (M7).** `slice_report` findings follow Orca's send dialog: the bed-temperature advisory (which Orca never shows) and the older A-series copy of the traditional-timelapse warning are dropped, and the timelapse warnings carry `appliesWhen: "timelapse"` and are not critical, since a G-code export records no timelapse. An `export_file` card for G-code or a sliced 3MF names the slice's own warnings, and the result lists them as `sliceWarnings`. `project_save` refuses a path that is not a `.3mf` before the card and points at `export_file` (a live run tried to save the project over the G-code path). A refused call now names values outside a listed set and says when `project_open` got both `path` and `new`. The page's state push no longer shows the `project_open` that replaced the project inside the project it opened (the call is still live while it finishes; its record belongs to the closed session). Both adapters' instructions carry one shared sentence on how a print request goes (`kPrintJourneyGuidance`): record the intent, ask only consequential questions, record the plan and its assumptions with `plan_set` before changing the project, check the slice, export with `export_file`. The live regression (`--live-agent`) gained a plan stage: the real model queues two settings patches under one `planId`, the harness clicks the plan card's Approve all, and both apply in order with the second not stale.
441:**Final journey eval, 2026-09-17, Windows, fake Bambu A1 mini, twice.** The model is Orca's bundled Cali Cat, written out as an STL by `export_file` (28.5 × 28.5 × 35 mm, one object).
