commit 2f4c6e6ef9f4a75ace7d741c47afca275d66d0cb
Author: Kenneth Jiang <k@obico.io>
Date:   Thu Sep 17 02:59:42 2026 +0000

    Run the workspace adapter harness on Windows

    The harness failed on Windows (select_object false after load_files, then
    0xC0000005). The cause was the machine, not stale test code:
    provision-mesa-windows.ps1 put the Mesa opengl32.dll only in mesa\, where
    only the app launcher loads it. A harness has no launcher, so it ran on the
    - finish() called ExitMainLoop(), skipping MainFrame::shutdown(); a backup
      posted soon after a model load then reached a deleted frame. It now
      closes the frame, as the shell harness does.

    The harness also stops with a message naming the provisioning script when
    the canvas has not initialized GL after the first load, instead of failing
    unrelated checks and crashing.

    Verified on the Windows machine with a separate build tree: 617 checks,
    exit 0, in repeated runs; with the harness's opengl32.dll removed, exit 1
    with the new error and no crash.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
--
+Pulling the script change does not fix a build tree that was already provisioned; run the script on it again. So that a tree without GL cannot fail this way unnoticed again, the harness now checks, right after its first model load, that the canvas initialized GL. If it did not, the harness stops with `HARNESS ERROR the 3D canvas did not initialize OpenGL` and the command to fix it, exits 1, and does not crash. A clean exit on that early path also needed the harness to close the main frame, as the shell harness does. It used to call `ExitMainLoop()`, which skips `MainFrame::shutdown()`, and a pending project backup then posted to the deleted frame.
